1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,296
Diya has zero rotis to
share among zero friends.

2
00:00:03,296 --> 00:00:05,178
How many does each friend get?

3
00:00:05,578 --> 00:00:06,478
Five?

4
00:00:06,168 --> 00:00:07,068
Ten?

5
00:00:06,705 --> 00:00:11,729
Any number works, because zero friends
times anything is still zero rotis.

6
00:00:11,729 --> 00:00:14,331
Zero over zero has no single answer.

7
00:00:14,781 --> 00:00:18,034
Diya thought about this in the
kitchen, helping with dinner.

8
00:00:18,384 --> 00:00:21,707
So when a limit gives zero
over zero, is it stuck?

9
00:00:21,707 --> 00:00:22,635
Not at all.

10
00:00:22,635 --> 00:00:24,690
It is a signal to look closer.

11
00:00:25,190 --> 00:00:29,215
Today we learn how, with limits of
polynomial and rational functions.

12
00:00:29,865 --> 00:00:31,516
Start with the easy kind.

13
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:37,038
A polynomial is made of whole number
powers of x, like x squared plus three

14
00:00:37,038 --> 00:00:38,147
x minus one.

15
00:00:38,547 --> 00:00:40,571
Its graph is one smooth curve.

16
00:00:40,571 --> 00:00:42,786
No holes, no jumps, no breaks.

17
00:00:43,186 --> 00:00:46,225
So its limit at a is just its value at a.

18
00:00:46,225 --> 00:00:48,289
Plug in, and you are done.

19
00:00:48,289 --> 00:00:53,125
As x tends to two, this one tends
to four plus six minus one, which is

20
00:00:53,125 --> 00:00:54,025
nine.

21
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:58,461
A rational function is one
polynomial divided by another.

22
00:00:58,461 --> 00:01:00,460
g of x, over h of x.

23
00:01:00,860 --> 00:01:04,233
If the bottom is not
zero at a, plug in again.

24
00:01:04,233 --> 00:01:06,883
The limit is g of a, over h of a.

25
00:01:07,283 --> 00:01:12,148
If the bottom is zero but the top
is not, like one over x minus one at

26
00:01:12,148 --> 00:01:14,179
one, the values shoot off.

27
00:01:14,179 --> 00:01:15,079
No limit.

28
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:18,807
And if the top is also
zero, we get zero over zero.

29
00:01:18,807 --> 00:01:21,062
That is where the real work begins.

30
00:01:21,712 --> 00:01:26,371
Take x squared minus one, over
x minus one, as x tends to one.

31
00:01:26,371 --> 00:01:28,769
Plugging in gives zero over zero.

32
00:01:29,169 --> 00:01:31,597
Watch both parts as x creeps towards one.

33
00:01:31,597 --> 00:01:33,344
The top shrinks towards zero.

34
00:01:33,344 --> 00:01:34,602
So does the bottom.

35
00:01:35,002 --> 00:01:36,591
But look at their ratio.

36
00:01:36,591 --> 00:01:38,970
It settles near two, and stays there.

37
00:01:39,370 --> 00:01:42,600
Top and bottom shrink
together, at a steady ratio.

38
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,849
That is why zero over zero
can still have a limit.

39
00:01:46,499 --> 00:01:47,399
Why two?

40
00:01:47,227 --> 00:01:49,714
Here is the way out, in small steps.

41
00:01:50,064 --> 00:01:50,964
Step one.

42
00:01:50,891 --> 00:01:55,338
The top is zero at one, so x
minus one is a factor of the top.

43
00:01:55,338 --> 00:01:57,254
That is the factor theorem.

44
00:01:58,004 --> 00:01:58,904
Step two.

45
00:01:58,876 --> 00:02:03,371
x squared minus one splits into
x minus one, times x plus one.

46
00:02:04,121 --> 00:02:05,067
Step three.

47
00:02:05,067 --> 00:02:08,955
Near one, x is not one, so
x minus one is not zero.

48
00:02:08,955 --> 00:02:10,333
We can cancel it.

49
00:02:11,083 --> 00:02:11,983
Step four.

50
00:02:11,905 --> 00:02:13,814
What is left is x plus one.

51
00:02:13,814 --> 00:02:14,788
Now plug in.

52
00:02:14,788 --> 00:02:16,253
One plus one is two.

53
00:02:16,903 --> 00:02:17,918
Now you try.

54
00:02:17,918 --> 00:02:21,991
x squared minus four, over x
minus two, as x tends to two.

55
00:02:21,991 --> 00:02:23,007
Is it stuck?

56
00:02:23,007 --> 00:02:24,269
Pause and decide.

57
00:02:27,469 --> 00:02:28,369
Not stuck!

58
00:02:28,327 --> 00:02:29,747
Cancel x minus two.

59
00:02:29,747 --> 00:02:31,887
Then x plus two tends to four.

60
00:02:32,537 --> 00:02:35,696
NCERT also gives a shortcut
that saves a lot of factoring.

61
00:02:36,096 --> 00:02:41,751
The limit of x to the n, minus a to
the n, over x minus a, as x tends to

62
00:02:41,751 --> 00:02:45,330
a, equals n times a to
the power n minus one.

63
00:02:46,080 --> 00:02:49,050
Check it with n equals
two and a equals one.

64
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:50,609
Two times one is two.

65
00:02:50,609 --> 00:02:52,254
That matches our answer.

66
00:02:52,904 --> 00:02:53,804
Quick one.

67
00:02:53,743 --> 00:02:57,676
x cubed minus eight, over x
minus two, as x tends to two.

68
00:02:57,676 --> 00:02:58,910
Six, or twelve?

69
00:02:58,910 --> 00:03:00,096
Pause and think.

70
00:03:03,296 --> 00:03:04,196
Twelve!

71
00:03:03,845 --> 00:03:05,661
Here n is three and a is two.

72
00:03:05,661 --> 00:03:07,585
Three times two squared is twelve.

73
00:03:08,235 --> 00:03:09,288
Remember this.

74
00:03:09,288 --> 00:03:11,268
Zero over zero is not stuck.

75
00:03:11,268 --> 00:03:13,434
Factor, cancel, then plug in.

76
00:03:14,134 --> 00:03:15,825
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:16,175 --> 00:03:21,099
Find the limit of x squared minus
five x plus six, over x squared minus

78
00:03:21,099 --> 00:03:23,251
nine, as x tends to three.

79
00:03:23,751 --> 00:03:24,651
Step one.

80
00:03:24,591 --> 00:03:25,749
Plug in three.

81
00:03:25,749 --> 00:03:28,965
The top is nine minus
fifteen plus six, zero.

82
00:03:28,965 --> 00:03:30,440
The bottom is zero.

83
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:31,547
So we factor.

84
00:03:32,297 --> 00:03:33,200
Step two.

85
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,670
The top is x minus two,
times x minus three.

86
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:40,360
The bottom is x minus
three, times x plus three.

87
00:03:41,110 --> 00:03:42,113
Step three.

88
00:03:42,113 --> 00:03:43,792
Cancel x minus three.

89
00:03:43,792 --> 00:03:46,833
We get x minus two, over x plus three.

90
00:03:47,583 --> 00:03:48,483
Step four.

91
00:03:48,446 --> 00:03:49,568
Plug in three.

92
00:03:49,568 --> 00:03:50,640
One over six.

93
00:03:51,290 --> 00:03:52,752
Now, the exam trap.

94
00:03:53,102 --> 00:03:57,586
x cubed minus eight, over x squared
minus four, as x tends to two.

95
00:03:57,586 --> 00:04:01,602
Plugging in gives zero over zero,
and many students write one.

96
00:04:02,002 --> 00:04:04,563
But zero over zero is not a number.

97
00:04:04,563 --> 00:04:06,051
Cancel x minus two.

98
00:04:06,051 --> 00:04:10,472
The top leaves x squared plus
two x plus four, which is twelve.

99
00:04:10,472 --> 00:04:13,075
The bottom leaves x plus two, four.

100
00:04:13,075 --> 00:04:14,614
The answer is three.

101
00:04:15,264 --> 00:04:16,795
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:16,795 --> 00:04:21,677
In JEE Main, as in September twenty
twenty: a sum of powers of x, over

103
00:04:21,677 --> 00:04:24,124
x minus one, as x tends to one.

104
00:04:24,724 --> 00:04:25,911
Exam level now.

105
00:04:25,911 --> 00:04:27,148
Find this limit.

106
00:04:27,148 --> 00:04:28,385
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:33,785 --> 00:04:35,158
The answer is A.

108
00:04:35,158 --> 00:04:36,058
Eight.

109
00:04:36,307 --> 00:04:37,493
Split the top.

110
00:04:37,493 --> 00:04:42,816
x to the five, plus x cubed, minus
two, is x to the five minus one, plus

111
00:04:42,816 --> 00:04:44,275
x cubed minus one.

112
00:04:44,775 --> 00:04:47,082
So the fraction splits
into two shortcut limits.

113
00:04:47,582 --> 00:04:50,071
The first gives five times one, five.

114
00:04:50,071 --> 00:04:52,703
The second gives three times one, three.

115
00:04:52,703 --> 00:04:54,413
Five plus three is eight.

116
00:04:55,013 --> 00:04:56,553
Here is your recap.

117
00:04:56,553 --> 00:04:58,135
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:01,335 --> 00:05:03,517
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:03,917 --> 00:05:04,817
One.

120
00:05:04,426 --> 00:05:08,458
What is the limit of x cubed
plus one, as x tends to two?

121
00:05:09,358 --> 00:05:10,258
Two.

122
00:05:09,752 --> 00:05:12,227
What should you do when
you get zero over zero?

123
00:05:13,127 --> 00:05:14,027
Three.

124
00:05:13,707 --> 00:05:18,486
What is the limit of x to the five
minus one, over x minus one, as x tends

125
00:05:18,486 --> 00:05:19,386
to one?

126
00:05:20,270 --> 00:05:23,503
Next time: sine x over x, when x is tiny.

127
00:05:23,503 --> 00:05:26,141
Why does it close in on exactly one?

128
00:05:26,141 --> 00:05:29,562
We find out with limits of
trigonometric functions.
